    {
      "id": "u2-valuation",
      "title": "Valuation basics",
      "summary": "What you pay for profits and cash flow.",
      "order": 2,
      "lessons": [
        {
          "id": "u2-l1",
          "title": "The P/E ratio",
          "xp": 15,
          "intro": "# Price-to-earnings\n\n**P/E** compares a share's price with the profit per share (EPS) behind it.\n\n- P/E = price / diluted EPS\n- If EPS is zero or negative, P/E is not meaningful (we show \"—\").\n\nA P/E of 20 means you pay $20 for each $1 of this year's earnings. It is a description, not a verdict.",
          "questions": [
            {
              "id": "q-u2l1-1",
              "type": "numeric",
              "prompt": "Costco's sample price is $920 and diluted EPS is $18.21. What is its P/E?",
              "answer": 50.52,
              "tolerance": 0.5,
              "unit": "multiple",
              "explanation": "P/E = 920 / 18.21 = 50.5x.",
              "source": {
                "ticker": "COST",
                "fy": 2025,
                "metrics": [
                  "pe"
                ],
                "formula": "price / eps_diluted"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "q-u2l1-2",
              "type": "true_false",
              "prompt": "Ford's EPS was $-0.5. True or false: Ford has a meaningful P/E this year.",
              "answer": false,
              "unit": "none",
              "explanation": "With negative earnings, price / EPS gives a negative number that tells you nothing useful, so P/E is shown as \"—\".",
              "source": {
                "ticker": "F",
                "fy": 2025,
                "metrics": [
                  "pe",
                  "eps_diluted"
                ],
                "formula": "price / eps_diluted"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "q-u2l1-3",
              "type": "multiple_choice",
              "prompt": "Microsoft trades at 37.4x earnings. What's its earnings yield (EPS / price)?",
              "choices": [
                "2.7%",
                "37.4%",
                "0.6%",
                "3.8%"
              ],
              "answer": 0,
              "unit": "percent",
              "explanation": "Earnings yield is the flip side of P/E: 1 / 37.4x ≈ 2.7%.",
              "source": {
                "ticker": "MSFT",
                "fy": 2025,
                "metrics": [
                  "earnings_yield"
                ],
                "formula": "eps_diluted / price"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "q-u2l1-4",
              "type": "compare",
              "prompt": "Which has the lower P/E (you pay less per $1 of earnings)?",
              "choices": [
                "KO",
                "NVDA"
              ],
              "answer": 0,
              "unit": "multiple",
              "explanation": "KO 23.0x vs NVDA 42.0x. A lower P/E is not automatically \"better\" — growth expectations differ.",
              "source": {
                "ticker": "KO",
                "fy": 2025,
                "metrics": [
                  "pe"
                ],
                "formula": "price / eps_diluted"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "q-u2l1-5",
              "type": "order",
              "prompt": "Rank by P/E, lowest first.",
              "choices": [
                "MSFT",
                "TGT",
                "AAPL"
              ],
              "answer": [
                1,
                2,
                0
              ],
              "unit": "multiple",
              "explanation": "TGT 10.7x, AAPL 34.1x, MSFT 37.4x.",
              "source": {
                "ticker": "TGT",
                "fy": 2025,
                "metrics": [
                  "pe"
                ],
                "formula": "price / eps_diluted"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "u2-l2",
          "title": "Free cash flow",
          "xp": 15,
          "intro": "# Cash that is actually free\n\nProfits are an accounting number. **Free cash flow (FCF)** is the cash left after running the business *and* investing in it.\n\n- FCF = operating cash flow − capital expenditures\n- FCF yield = FCF / market cap\n\nHeavy builders (chip fabs, factories) can show big profits but small FCF.",
          "questions": [
            {
              "id": "q-u2l2-1",
              "type": "numeric",
              "prompt": "Micron: operating cash flow $17.5B, capex $15.9B. Free cash flow in $B?",
              "answer": 1600000000,
              "tolerance": 50000000,
              "unit": "usd",
              "explanation": "FCF = $17.5B − $15.9B = $1.6B. Building fabs eats most of the cash.",
              "source": {
                "ticker": "MU",
                "fy": 2025,
                "metrics": [
                  "free_cash_flow"
                ],
                "formula": "operating_cash_flow - capex"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "q-u2l2-2",
              "type": "multiple_choice",
              "prompt": "Apple's FCF is $98.8B on $416.2B revenue. FCF margin?",
              "choices": [
                "26.9%",
                "2.6%",
                "46.9%",
                "23.7%"
              ],
              "answer": 3,
              "unit": "percent",
              "explanation": "FCF margin = $98.8B / $416.2B = 23.7%.",
              "source": {
                "ticker": "AAPL",
                "fy": 2025,
                "metrics": [
                  "fcf_margin"
                ],
                "formula": "free_cash_flow / revenue"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "q-u2l2-3",
              "type": "true_false",
              "prompt": "True or false: Micron's free cash flow was larger than its net income in FY2025.",
              "answer": false,
              "unit": "none",
              "explanation": "FCF $1.6B vs net income $8.5B. Capex is a cash cost that doesn't hit profit all at once.",
              "source": {
                "ticker": "MU",
                "fy": 2025,
                "metrics": [
                  "free_cash_flow",
                  "net_income"
                ],
                "formula": "operating_cash_flow - capex"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "q-u2l2-4",
              "type": "compare",
              "prompt": "Which has the higher FCF yield (FCF / market cap)?",
              "choices": [
                "F",
                "MSFT"
              ],
              "answer": 0,
              "unit": "percent",
              "explanation": "Ford 18.6% vs Microsoft 1.9%. A high yield can reflect low expectations as much as cheapness.",
              "source": {
                "ticker": "F",
                "fy": 2025,
                "metrics": [
                  "fcf_yield"
                ],
                "formula": "free_cash_flow / market_cap"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "q-u2l2-5",
              "type": "order",
              "prompt": "Rank by FCF margin, highest first.",
              "choices": [
                "COST",
                "NVDA",
                "KO"
              ],
              "answer": [
                1,
